# 020_e2_ramanujan_10_691.oc -- [E] cl:ramanujan (Claim 8.1) at (10, 691),
# from the console (slot-5 WP-10 item 3; [X] Sec. 7's `ext_10_691.oc`, as far
# as the C1b binding surface reaches).
#
# This is phase E2's flagship case: the congruence tau(q) == sigma_11(q)
# mod 691 of Ramanujan, read off the Hecke action on H^1(Gamma; Sym^10) mod 691.
# `HeckeExtRamanujanValidation` asserts the whole `hecke_ext/v1` record for it;
# what is reachable from the console today is the record's BASIS-INDEPENDENT
# arithmetic content, and that is what this script pins.
#
# NOT HERE, and why: the record object itself (`HeckeExtDriver`, the local
# algebra A_S = F_691[eps]/eps^2, the Loewy and socle profiles, the generation
# verdict) is phase C5's, which [P] Sec. 10 gates on E5's record-schema freeze.
# Binding it now would freeze a printer for a record shape that is still moving
# -- the same mistake C1b avoided against E0b, one layer up.
#
# THE FOUR IDENTITIES ARE EXACT IDENTITIES IN M_3(F_691), not eigenvalue
# statements: each says that a whole 3x3 matrix is a scalar multiple of
# T_2 + 24, which is a statement about the nilpotent part as well.

include("prelude.oc");

H = cohomology(symmetric_power(MODEL, N_SYM, ELL));
assert(H.dims[1] == 3, "dim H^1(Gamma; Sym^10 (x) F_691) = 3");
assert(H.dims[0] == 0, "and H^0 vanishes off the Dickson locus");
assert(H.dims[2] == 0, "and H^2 vanishes on this model");
D = 3;
I = identity(ELL, D);

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (1) The eigenvalues are DERIVED from Ramanujan's congruence, not copied.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# tau(q) == sigma_11(q) = 1 + q^11  (mod 691) for every prime q.  So the
# eigenvalue of T_q on the cuspidal line is computable here with integer
# arithmetic alone, and the four numbers 667, 252, 684, 531 of [E] Claim 8.1
# are its output rather than its input.

A2 = hecke(H, 2).A(1);
A3 = hecke(H, 3).A(1);
A5 = hecke(H, 5).A(1);
A7 = hecke(H, 7).A(1);
a2 = (2^11 + 1) % ELL;
a3 = (3^11 + 1) % ELL;
a5 = (5^11 + 1) % ELL;
a7 = (7^11 + 1) % ELL;
assert([a2, a3, a5, a7] == [667, 252, 684, 531],
       "the four eigenvalues of [E] Claim 8.1, derived from sigma_11");

# T_q - a_q is nilpotent of Jordan type (2, 1): the 691-congruence makes the
# Eisenstein and the cuspidal reductions share one eigenvalue, so the whole of
# H^1 is a single generalised eigenspace -- and it is NOT semisimple.
for cell in [[2, a2], [3, a3], [5, a5], [7, a7]] {
    q = cell[0];
    aq = cell[1];
    Aq = hecke(H, q).A(1);
    assert(jordan_type(sub(Aq, scale(I, aq))) == [2, 1],
           "T_" + str(q) + " - " + str(aq) + " has Jordan type (2, 1)");
    assert(rank(sub(Aq, scale(I, aq))) == 1,
           "so it has rank 1 -- two Jordan blocks on a 3-dimensional space");
    assert(trace(Aq) == (3 * aq) % ELL, "and its trace is 3 a_q");
}
print([a2, a3, a5, a7]);

# The same for the bad prime: tau(691) == sigma_11(691) == 1 (mod 691), and the
# U_691 eigenvalue is therefore 1.  This is the strongest single test in [E] --
# it exercises U_ell and the coset construction at ell = p.
U = hecke(H, ELL).A(1);
assert(jordan_type(sub(U, I)) == [2, 1], "U_691 - 1 has Jordan type (2, 1)");
assert(trace(U) == 3, "tau(691) == 1 (mod 691), three times over");

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (2) The four exact identities of [E] cl:ramanujan, in M_3(F_691).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   3 (T_3   -   252) = 256 (T_2 + 24)
#   3 (T_5   -  4830) = 174 (T_2 + 24)
#   3 (T_7   + 16744) = 137 (T_2 + 24)
#   3 (U_691 -     1) = -542 (T_2 + 24)
#
# The right-hand side is the SAME matrix every time, so the four say that the
# local algebra A_S is generated by T_2 alone -- generation certificate (a)'s
# statement, and the reason [E] Claim 2.11 reads `T_2 alone generates`.

BASE = add(A2, scale(I, 24));
assert(scale(sub(A3, scale(I, 252 % ELL)), 3) == scale(BASE, 256),
       "3 (T_3 - 252) = 256 (T_2 + 24)");
assert(scale(sub(A5, scale(I, 4830 % ELL)), 3) == scale(BASE, 174),
       "3 (T_5 - 4830) = 174 (T_2 + 24)");
assert(scale(add(A7, scale(I, 16744 % ELL)), 3) == scale(BASE, 137),
       "3 (T_7 + 16744) = 137 (T_2 + 24)");
assert(scale(sub(U, I), 3) == scale(BASE, ELL - 542),
       "3 (U_691 - 1) = -542 (T_2 + 24)");
print([256, 174, 137, ELL - 542]);

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (3) The one local block, and its algebra.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# E1's Stage 3 builds A_S on a block that E0 has certified local.  Here there is
# exactly one: H^1 is a single maximal ideal with residue field F_691, and the
# minimal polynomial (X - 667)^2 is what makes A_S = F_691[eps]/eps^2 --
# Gorenstein, embedding dimension 1, Loewy = socle = [2, 1].

DEC = decompose(hecke(H, 2), 1);
assert(DEC.num_blocks == 1, "one maximal ideal at (10, 691)");
B = DEC.blocks[0];
assert(B.local, "certified local");
assert(B.dim == 3, "the whole of H^1");
assert(B.residue_degree == 1, "with residue field F_691 itself");
assert(B.jordan(2) == [2, 1], "the block's Jordan partition is (2, 1)");
assert(degree(B.min_poly(2)) == 2, "the minimal polynomial is quadratic");
assert(degree(B.char_poly(2)) == 3, "the characteristic polynomial is cubic");
assert(B.witness_poly == poly(ELL, [24, 1]),
       "the maximal ideal is (T_2 + 24) = (T_2 - 667)");
assert(coeffs(B.min_poly(2)) == coeffs(mul(B.witness_poly, B.witness_poly)),
       "and the minimal polynomial is its SQUARE -- A_S = F_691[eps]/eps^2");
assert(DEC.certify() == 0, "and the decomposition re-derives");
print(B.dim, B.residue_degree, B.jordan(2), B.min_poly(2), B.witness_poly);

# The golden pins, so the matrices above are the reference's own.
for q in [2, 3, 5] {
    assert(hash(hecke(H, q).A(1)) ==
               golden("n=10 ell=691 p=" + str(q) + " r=1", "A(T_p)^1").hash,
           "A(T_" + str(q) + ")^1 is the golden matrix");
}
assert(hash(DEC) == golden(POINT_HECKE, "decomp_A(T_p)^1_invariants").hash,
       "decomp_A(T_p)^1_invariants");

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# (4) One BASIS-DEPENDENT pin, labelled as such.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A^{(1)}(U_691) = I + 221 E_31 is [E] Claim 8.1's "Verified" paragraph.  Unlike
# everything above it depends on the STORED H^1 basis, which is a golden tier-B
# object (`decomp_*_U`), so it moves with a tier-B re-baseline and with nothing
# else.  It is asserted, not printed, for exactly that reason.
assert(U == add(I, matrix(ELL, [[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [221, 0, 0]])),
       "A^{(1)}(U_691) = I + 221 E_31 in the stored basis (BASIS-DEPENDENT)");

assert(fingerprint() == "0x0000000000000000", "every pin taken at the defaults");
annotate("020_e2_ramanujan_10_691: 4 identities, 5 eigenvalues, 1 local block");
